  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$n$ is less than $10$ **or** $13$, **and** $n$ is an integer\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $X(n):$ $n$ is less than $10$\n\n- $Y(n):$ $n$ is less than $13$\n\n- $Z(n):$ $n$ is an integer",
  "worked_solution": "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:  \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$ is less than $10$ or $13.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{n\\text{ is less than }13}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∨Y.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is an integer. This corresponds to $Z.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10\\text{ or }13}_{⏟}}_{X∨Y}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{n\\text{ is an integer}}_{⏟}}_{Z},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(X∨Y)∧Z.$",
